---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DocsTable, Callout } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const ROLES = [
  ['NEXUS', 'Orchestrator', 'Yes', 'Owns the whole pipeline: picks up issues, assigns work, coordinates the team, recovers from failures, and notifies humans when needed.'],
  ['FORGE', 'Builder', 'No', 'Writes code against an agreed plan, creates branches, and opens pull requests.'],
  ['SENTINEL', 'Reviewer', 'Yes', 'Adversarially reviews plans and code for security, quality, and test coverage.'],
  ['VESSEL', 'DevOps', 'No', 'Watches CI, resolves merge conflicts, and merges green pull requests.'],
  ['LORE', 'Writer', 'No', 'Documents decisions and updates changelogs after a merge. Often disabled by default.'],
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' }, { label: 'Agents' }]}>
  <h1>The Team: agents and roles</h1>

  <p>
    OpenFlows runs a group of AI agents that cooperate like a real engineering team. Each agent has one primary job,
    and together they close the loop from issue to merged pull request.
  </p>

  <h2>The five roles</h2>
  <DocsTable headers={['Agent', 'Role', 'Reviews before acting?', 'What it does']} rows={ROLES} />

  <h2>How they act like a team</h2>
  <p>The team is sequential where it must be and parallel where it can be:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NEXUS plans the assignment</strong> and hands work to an idle builder.</li>
    <li><strong>FORGE writes and stops</strong> until its plan is approved.</li>
    <li><strong>SENTINEL reviews</strong> — but never touches FORGE's workspace.</li>
    <li><strong>VESSEL merges</strong> and tears down the workspace.</li>
    <li><strong>LORE documents</strong> the completed work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Roles are configured, not hard-coded</h2>
  <p>
    Which roles exist, how many workers each role can run at once, what model each uses, and what skills and tools are
    available — all of this lives in the <strong>agent registry</strong> in the control plane and can be changed
    without a restart or redeploy.
  </p>

  <h2>What an agent workspace looks like</h2>
  <p>Each agent runs in its own isolated, disposable workspace:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No LLM API keys.</li>
    <li>No raw repository credentials.</li>
    <li>Heavily restricted network access (control plane, GitHub, coordination store — and little else).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The agent coordinates with the team strictly through a small, typed command surface — it never talks directly
    to the coordination store or the machine plane. This is what keeps the whole system safe and auditable.
  </p>

  <h2>Agent directory</h2>

  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
    <a href="/docs/architecture/orchestrator" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-bone); border: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 1.5rem;">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--color-agent-nexus);">NEXUS  -  Orchestrator</h3>
        <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--color-graphite);">The brain. Ingests issues, assigns work, coordinates the team, recovers from failures, and escalates to humans.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a href="/docs/architecture/agents/forge" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-bone); border: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 1.5rem;">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--color-agent-forge);">FORGE  -  Builder</h3>
        <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--color-graphite);">Writes code against an agreed plan, creates branches, and opens pull requests. Halts until the plan is approved.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a href="/docs/architecture/agents/sentinel" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-bone); border: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 1.5rem;">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--color-agent-sentinel);">SENTINEL  -  Reviewer</h3>
        <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--color-graphite);">Adversarially reviews plans and code from an isolated workspace. Approves gates and requests evidence.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a href="/docs/architecture/agents/vessel" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-bone); border: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 1.5rem;">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--color-agent-vessel);">VESSEL  -  DevOps</h3>
        <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--color-graphite);">Watches CI, resolves merge conflicts, merges approved pull requests, and tears down workspaces.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
    <a href="/docs/architecture/agents/lore" style="text-decoration: none; color: inherit;">
      <div style="background: var(--color-bone); border: 1px solid var(--color-driftwood); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 1.5rem;">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: var(--color-agent-lore);">LORE  -  Writer</h3>
        <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0; color: var(--color-graphite);">Documents decisions and updates changelogs after a merge. Often disabled by default.</p>
      </div>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>Adding to the team</h2>
  <p>
    You can extend what the team can do <strong>without writing code</strong> by adding skills, tools, or models
    through the registry. Adding a brand-new <em>role</em> is the one extension that touches the system internally.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
